From 81f426a8573df30a619fb4d1ecca293c089ac9bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 01:22:25 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(js): fire scroll events in the rendering update's scroll
 steps

A scroll fired its event from a setTimeout(0) of its own: per element, plus a
second one at the document and window for a viewport scroll, plus a third from
the CDP wheel path, and the MCP scroll tool dispatched two more by hand. HTML
queues the scroll target once in the document's pending scroll event targets
and fires the events in the next update of the rendering, before animation
frame callbacks.

The engine already runs one rendering opportunity for animation frames,
ResizeObserver and IntersectionObserver. Scroll targets now queue there
(deduplicated, in order) and fire first; scrolling the viewport's root element
fires at the document, whose event bubbles to the window. The CDP wheel path
and the MCP scroll tool no longer dispatch scroll events of their own. Tests
read their counters from an animation frame callback, which is where a page
can first see the event, as in Chrome.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tm2SNEJ42hMPiZ6cFb8Vih
